===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷeys-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|cure}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|cure|t=care, cure, healing, cure of souls}}, from {{der|en|la|cura||care, medical attendance, cure}}. Displaced native {{noncog|ang|hǣlu}}, but this survived as {{m|en|heal}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[method]], [[device]] or [[medication]] that [[restore]]s good [[health]].
#: {{syn|en|curative|mithridate|treacle}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=5|passage=When you're well enough off so's you don't have to fret about anything but your heft or your diseases you begin to get queer, I suppose. And the queerer the '''cure''' for those ailings the bigger the attraction. A place like the Right Livers' Rest was bound to draw freaks, same as molasses draws flies.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=Marilyn McLeod; Pamela Sawyer|title=w:Love Hangover|artist=w:Diana Ross|year=1976|passage='Cause if there's a '''cure''' for this, I don't want it / I don't want it}}
# An act of healing or state of being healed; [[restoration]] to [[health]] after a [[disease]], or to [[soundness]] after [[injury]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|act=IV|scene=i|passage=Past hope! past '''cure'''!}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Luke|13|32|passage=I do '''cures''' to-day and to-morrow.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Aislaibie Denham|title=The Denham Tracts|year=1892|volume=2|page=50|text=A disease among cattle, called the murrain, then prevailed to a very great extent through that district of Yorkshire. The cattle were made to pass through the smoke raised by this miraculous fire, and their '''cure''' was looked upon as certain.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[solution]] to a [[problem]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|passage=Cold, hunger, prisons, ills without a '''cure'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1763|author={{w|Richard Hurd (bishop)|Richard Hurd}}|title= On the Uses of Foreign Travel
|passage=the proper '''cure''' of such prejudices}}
# A process of [[preservation]], as by [[smoking]].
# Cured fish.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|title=Cod|author=Mark Kurlansky|page=128|text=Well into the twentieth century, Lunenburg, Nova Scotia's Grand Banks fleet stayed with sail power. "The Lunenburg '''cure'''," heavily salted on the schooners and then dried on flakes along the rocky sheltered coastline, was traded in the Caribbean.}}
# A process of [[solidification]] or [[gelling]].
# {{lb|en|engineering}} A [[process]] whereby a material is caused to form permanent [[molecular]] linkages by [[exposure]] to chemicals, heat, pressure or [[weathering]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Care, heed, or attention.
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|passage=vicarages of great '''cure''', but small value}}
# Spiritual charge; care of soul; the office of a parish priest or of a [[curate]].
#* {{RQ:Skelton Complete|Hauke|61|lines=1–4, 25–26|This worke devysed is<br>For suche as do amys,<br>And specyally to controule<br>Such as have '''cure''' of soule, {{...}}<br>No good priest to offende,<br>But suche dawes to amend, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Spelman De Non Temerandis Ecclesiis|edition=6th|chapter=Clement Spelman Preface|page=4|passage=[T]he Appropriator was the incumbent Parſon, and had the '''Cure''' of the Souls of the Pariſhioners, and that upon the Preſentation of the Appropriation, or upon the Diſſolution of the Abbey, the Church became void, and preſentative, as other Churches upon Reſignation, or Death of the Incumbent.}}
#* {{RQ:Goldsmith Vicar of Wakefield|chapter=III|passage=During this interval, my thoughts were employed on some future means of supporting them; and at last a small '''Cure''' of fifteen pounds a year was offered me in a distant neighbourhood, where I could still enjoy my principles without molestation.}}
