====Verb====
{{wikidata lexeme|L1377}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move with rhythmic steps or movements, especially in time to music.
#: {{ux|en|I '''danced''' with her all night long.}}
#: {{ux|en|These drum beats are making me '''dance'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=“Well,” I answered, at first with uncertainty, then with inspiration, “he would do splendidly to lead your cotillon, if you think of having one.” &para; “So you do not '''dance''', Mr. Crocker?” &para; I was somewhat set back by her perspicuity.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|intransitive}} To leap or move lightly and rapidly.
#: {{ux|en|His eyes '''danced''' with pleasure as he spoke.&nbsp;&nbsp; She accused her political opponent of '''dancing''' around the issue instead of confronting it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1812–1818|author=[[w:Lord Byron|George Gordon [Lord] Byron]]|title=w:Childe Harold's Pilgrimage|others=canto 2, verse 54|passage=And woods along the banks are waving high, / Whose shadows in the glassy waters '''dance''',}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|album=|author=w:Ecstasy, Passion & Pain|title=One Beautiful Day|text=Sweet visions of you / Will '''dance''' through my head / Tonight while I lie / Awake in my bed}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 November 15|author=Prof. Jim Wild|title=This train was delayed because of bad weather in space|journal=RAIL|issue=996|page=30|text=A common and beautiful side-effect of high solar activity is the Aurora Borealis - the northern lights that '''dance''' across Arctic skies.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To perform the steps to.
#: {{ux|en|Have you ever '''danced''' the tango?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to dance, or move nimbly or merrily about.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=II|scene=i|passage=Met we on hill, in dale, forest, or mead,<br>By pavèd fountain, or by rushy brook,<br>or in the beachèd margent of the sea,<br>To '''dance''' our ringlets to the whistling wind,<br>But with thy brawls thou hast disturbed our sport.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus|act=V|scene=iii|passage=Thy grandsire loved thee well; / Many a time he '''danced''' thee on his knee.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|euphemism}} To [[make love]] or [[have sex]].
#: {{ux|en|You make me feel like '''dancing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|apiology|of a worker honey bee}} To make a [[repetitive]] movement in order to [[communicate]] to other [[worker]] [[honey bee]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=It was seen that the readiness to '''dance''' and intensity of the dance are clearly increased when the temperature in the hive remained between 28-36° C.|title=An Understanding of Dancing Behaviour|work=The American Bee Journal|year=1961|month=November|author=W. Wittekindt|page=434|editor=G. H. Cale|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_american-bee-journal_1961-11_101_11/page/434|issue=11|volume=101|location=Hamilton|url=https://archive.org/details/sim_american-bee-journal_1961-11_101_11}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|euphemism}} To kick and convulse from the effects of being hanged.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|title=Literary Digest|volume=34|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Literary_Digest/ZIE4AQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Dance+upon+empty+air&dq=Dance+upon+empty+air&printsec=frontcover|page=364|passage=If that veil can be maintained, if the workers can be kept from knowing the perfidy of officials, the criminality of capitalism, the murderous vengeance that is planned by the plutocratic powers of America, then Charles Moyer, William D. Haywood, and George Pettibone will '''dance''' on empty air, while the ghouls of capitalism rejoice because they have landed another blow upon the body of resisting labor.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:Walter Noble Burns|title=The Saga of Billy the Kid|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Saga_of_Billy_the_Kid/793gmWRK4OIC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=death+dance+for+the+hangman&pg=PA250&printsec=frontcover|page=250|passage=Beneath the shoulder blades of the limp form lying there lay the heart that had hated him, that had beat high at the thought of seeing him kick at the end of a hangman's rope, that had exulted in the prospect of him '''dancing''' a death dance on air.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Paul Doherty|title=The Hangman's Hymn (Canterbury Tales Mysteries, Book 5)|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Hangman_s_Hymn_Canterbury_Tales_Myst/NXMEqWkYp9IC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=death+dance+for+the+hangman&pg=PT26&printsec=frontcover|passage=Simon had seen other men executed at Berkeley, both in the castle and outside in the village. They were usually strung up like rats, left to '''dance''', their death throes sometimes seeming to last forever.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2010-01-12|author=Sara Starbuck|title=Dread Pirate Fleur and the Hangman's Noose|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Dread_Pirate_Fleur_and_the_Hangman_s_Noo/dEvR5QN4uzgC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=death+dance+for+the+hangman&pg=PA342&printsec=frontcover|page=342|passage='Hats off!' the shout went up, not out of respect for those about to die, but for a better view of their death dance. The hangman, who was as drunk as anyone else,  uncoiled the rope's free end from each prisoner in turn and threw it up to an assistant balanced precariously on the beam above. Each was tightly fastened, leaving very little slack. When the moment came, the carts would be driven out from under the prisoners, leaving them '''dancing''' the Tyburn jig, their legs paddling helplessly in the air.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2019-11-07|author=Carter J. Gregory|title=The Hangman's Psalm: The Girl at the Gallows|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Hangman_s_Psalm_The_Girl_at_the_Gall/R9bHDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=dance+of+death+for+the+hangman&pg=PT235&printsec=frontcover|passage=Not only will you be whipped, but Jack Ketch will be displeased. He knows how to attach the rope just so...a man's neck can be snaped in an instant and he feels pain for a moment only. Or, at Ketch's pleasure, the man swings in the air, his legs '''dance''' in an ungainly manner, his face turns red, his tongue turns purple and protrudes - a poor devil once bit off the tip and spit it out, and he chocked(''sic'') to death.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2022-11-30|author=John Gardiner|title=A Hitchhiker's Triptych|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/A_Hitchhiker_s_Triptych/-pebEAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=death+dance+for+the+hangman&pg=PT522&printsec=frontcover|passage=Initially, all hanging deaths were by the short rope. The victims strangled as they performed a twitching death dance. Over time there was a move to a longer rope. The extra drop meant the victim's neck snapped, causing a more humane death. For a long time adter the long rope was introduced, stories circulated around Aberdeen of executioners slipping in the short rope for criminals convicted of particularly heinous crimes, especially for crimes against children. No quick end for these devils. A slow '''dancing''' death, courtesy of the short rope, brouht in by canny executioners.}}

